well the karate kid is pretty much a  perfect movie there's far more to this  film than sanding floors painting fences  beating up bad guys the behind the  scenes story is equally fascinating  let's take a look at why the Karate Kid  is the best around the saga of Daniel  Larusso and mr. Miyagi all started when  producer Jerry Weintraub saw a news  story about a local kid who earned a  black belt in karate to protect himself  from a group of bullies Weintraub was  immediately inspired and soon enough  Columbia Pictures hired Robert mark came  in to write a screenplay maybe you've  never heard Caymans name you've  certainly seen his movies  the man has written films like Lethal  Weapon 3 the pith elements the  transporter and taken and when he was  approached with the idea of a martial  arts movie came and was able to draw on  his personal experience while writing  the script after all he'd been studying  karate for 17 years he based the  character of mr. Miyagi on one of his  instructors while the character of John  Kriss was a combination of two people  came and had known in real life a tough  marine turned karate teacher and a  martial arts instructor who ordered his  pupils to injure their opponents here on  the street in competition a man  confronts you he's the enemy  other than maybe Yoda or obi-wan Kenobi  mr. Miyagi is probably the greatest  movie mentor of all time Pat Morita is  perfect in the role and his performance  earned him an Oscar nod for Best  Supporting Actor however back in the day  Morita wasn't such an obvious choice for  the parts of a martial arts master he  used to be a comedian and before the  Karate Kid Morita was best known for  playing the comical character of Arnold  on happy days so in director John G  Alton approached producer Jerry  Weintraub with the idea of casting  Marita's Miyagi Weintraub wouldn't even  consider it he was adamant he says I  didn't want a comedian  I'd want a comic actor for this once a  heavyweight part I want an actor  Weintraub wanted to go with legendary  Japanese actor Toshiro Mifune  but avildsen was convinced Morita was  the right man for the job so he went  ahead and did a script reading with  Morita and then surprised Weintraub with  the audition tape the producer was kind  of impressed although he required marina  to audition five more times before  giving him again  but after passing his fifth and final  test Morita finally nabbed the wall  [Music]  believe it or not Ralph Macchio was 22  when he played the part of teenage  Daniel Larusso  but despite the age gap Maggio made for  a perfect protagonist coming off of  Francis Ford Coppola's The Outsiders  Maggio had some steam heading into the  Karate Kid but when it came to landing  the role his snarky attitude is what  really helped him get the gig after  screenwriter Robert mark came in met  Maggio to see if he was right for the  role he came away describing the young  actor is obnoxious and really that's  exactly what came in in John G Appleton  wanted they were looking for somebody  with an attitude a guy who wouldn't take  crap from anyone just like Daniel in  fact they liked Macchio so much that  they kind of rewrote the part for him  originally his character was called  Daniel Weber  but since Maggio was Italian they  changed his last name to Larusso  as for William Zabka the actor who  played bully Johnny Lawrence he probably  got the job thanks to some extreme  method acting when he was first trying  out for the part sabka got so into  character that he actually grabbed  Appleton by the shirt which usually  isn't a smart idea when you're trying to  impress the director but in this case it  seems like Zab cos bad guy persona paid  off because he was cast as the captain  of the Cobra Kai creating one of the  most memorable villains that come out of  the 1980s when Ralph Macchio and company  signed up for the Karate Kid none of  them knew much about martial arts so to  get them ready for a karate tournament  the filmmaker called in Patti Johnson  this guy had studied Tang pseudo while  serving in Korea and when he returned to  America he joined up with Chuck Norris  working at one of Nora's schools soon  Johnson racked up an impressive fight  record and not only would he go on to  work with stars like Brandon Lee and  Jackie Chan he also had a part in Bruce  Lee's Enter the Dragon  in other words Johnson's combat skills  were as legit as they come and when he  was hired as karate kids fight  choreographer he got pretty creative  when it came to instructing the actors  for example he taught Macchio and Morita  together and he made sure to train them  in a very relaxed atmosphere similar to  mr. Miyagi style however Johnson took a  very different approach with a Cobra Kai  he basically put them through boot camp  forcing them to do push-ups and treating  them like soldiers as for the actor who  played crease Johnson explained on a DVD  special feature that he wanted  the kids to view Martin Cove as an  experienced black belt to create this  illusion Johnson trained Koch privately  peeping him away from the rest of the  bad guys that way when Cove met the rest  of the crew they would view him as a  real martial artist Johnson's methods  were pretty clever but his work on the  karate kid wasn't all just behind the  scenes the next time you watch the film  pay attention during the karate  tournament and you'll see the fight  choreographer playing the part of the  mustachioed referee while Johnny  Lawrence is the bad guy we all love to  hate john crease is the real villain of  the Karate Kid  crease is a Vietnam vet who believes in  striking first striking hard and showing  no mercy however many Karate Kid fans  have wondered what the movie would have  been like if the Cobra Kai sensei had  been played by Chuck Norris after all  Norris was offered the part but turned  it down  right yeah I heard that too well  according to CBO calm the Chuck Norris  connection is nothing more than a myth  as it turns out Norris was never offered  the role although if someone had asked  him to play crease Norris says he would  have passed on the part why because he  thought the character cast a bad light  on Karate so while it's fun to imagine  Walker Texas Ranger in the role we can  all agree that Martin Cove was the best  choice this is a karate dojo not a  knitting class you don't come in my dojo  and drop a challenge and leave old man  while pat morita through quite a few  punches in the Karate Kid he wasn't able  to pull off every move required by the  script so when it came time for mr.  Miyagi to do something crazy like jump  off a chain-link fence to give karate  lessons to some bully's stress the  skeletons Morita would step aside for a  real-life martial artist named Fumio de  Muro  de Muro was a regular on the cover of  black belt magazine and he performed in  places like Las Vegas and Paris  eventually demurrer befriended Chuck  Norris and it said the American  recommended him for the parts of mr.  Miyagi according to de Moura he turned  down the opportunity because his English  wasn't good enough although he did  accept the parts as Pat Morita stuntman  doubling for the actor in the first  three films of franchise but while de  Moura is the most famous stunt double in  the film we've got to give a shout-out  to Darrell the doll not only did but  doll play one of the semi-finalists in  the movie's climactic karate tournament  but he's also the guy who doubled for  Morita and the beach scene where Miyagi  froze his graceful crane kick basically  but all is the man who helps  the movies most famous scene so we've  got to give some credit where credit is  due the 1980s was a great decade for  motivational themed songs but few were  quite as inspiring as you're the best by  Joe Esposito this blood pumping anthem  plays during the montage sequence of the  karate kids tournament scene as Daniel  Larusso hits and kicks his way toward  the big showdown with Johnny Lawrence  the song is amazingly epic and  over-the-top but shockingly in some  alternate universe there's a version of  the karate kid that doesn't have it as  it turns out Esposito who would also  contribute to Flashdance and coming to  America originally wrote you're the best  for Rock III however Sylvester Stallone  decided to go with survivors eye of the  tiger instead fortunately for film fans  everywhere director John G Appleton  loved Esposito song and incorporated it  into a spill even though lines like  history repeats itself trying you'll  succeed probably would have fit veterans  to loan spell so basically Iraqis losses  are gain and the song is one of the big  reasons that we still love Esposito  today and being part of such an iconic  film was incredibly rewarding for  Esposito as well as the singer once  explained pop dose I'm thrilled to have  been a part of it it gives me a little  something back for all the years I've  put in don't forget Diamond Vegas  singing in lounges and sometimes it's a  little discouraging but when someone  remembers what I've done from the movie  it makes me feel pretty good it seems  like every time a movie rakes in the big  bucks it doesn't take long before  someone drags the filmmakers into court  unfortunately the Karate Kid is no  exception the trouble all started when a  karate instructor named Bill D Clemente  filed a lawsuit claiming the movie  ripped off his nickname D Clement e's  moniker was indeed the Karate Kid and he  even named his school the Karate Kid  dojo so when the movie came out D  Clemente assumed screenwriter Robert  mark came in had stolen his identity  however a New York courts just didn't  see it that way  sadly for buildi Clemente his case was  crane kicked right out of court when  most people think of the Karate Kid  they remember Daniels bizarre training  regimen or his final confrontation with  Johnny but in between all the fighting  there are a couple of scenes that packed  an emotional punch for example there's a  moment when Daniel finds a drunken mr.  Miyagi wearing a soldier's uniform while  singing a mournful song  so you're singing that jaboney brews hey  Daniel soon learns that his mentor  fought for America during World War two  but despite his patriotism his pregnant  wife was tossed into an internment camp  where she died due to complications  during birth it's a touching moment and  one that was largely inspired by Pat  Morita zone life growing up Morita spent  part of his childhood in an internment  camp for japanese-americans he remained  there for nearly two years of prisoner  who'd committed no crime so when it came  time to film this particular scene  Morita was instrumental in developing  Miyagi's backstory in fact according to  writer Charles C Gooden the song Miyagi  is singing is an actual tune he heard  while in prison amazingly the studio  initially wanted to cut it out but  Morita and John G Appleton stood their  ground and the director believes it was  this scene that earned Morita his Oscar  nominee check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the belt so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

